closest-possible: the decode loop matches the Crystal LZWDecode step for step (256 single-byte seeds, 16-bit code pairs, the three cases new_code < size / == next_code / otherwise error, same output and checksum), but dictionary phrases are (start, length) spans into one growing byte arena instead of Array(String) entries, because a phrase cannot be held as an owned growable byte string per entry in next-lang without a fixed capacity per entry. The untimed input is produced by an inline integer-pair LZW encoder rather than the Crystal LZWEncode class; for pure-ASCII input it emits the same codes, so the timed decode sees identical data. Prep mode runs only the warmup rounds.
